Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {In the search for space-efficient nature-based solutions (NBS) for
   stormwater management, we designed a vertical greenery system (VGS) for
   enhanced evapotranspiration (ET). After assessing a range of
   construction options, an 80 m long and 3.2 m high freestanding
   stormwater-VGS, referred to as the Green Climate Screen (GCS), was
   constructed in 2019 in Copenhagen. The GCS receives runoff from 240 m(2)
   of roof top and has a high ratio of surface-to-ground area to allow for
   the clothesline effect to enhance ET. The conveyance of runoff to the
   top of the GCS is based on gravitational force. It is estimated that 24
   h events with return periods up to the 0.1 y (13.9 mm) are managed by
   ET, from 0.1 to 15 y (48.5 mm) by infiltration beneath the screen, and
   from 15 to > 100 y by overflow to an adjacent area allowing for
   aboveground storage (167 mm), in total 229 mm. With the fulfillment of
   most performance criteria and successful inclusion of selected
   co-benefits ET-based stormwater, NBS could become future standard
   elements. To reduce CO2 and resource footprint, steel, concrete, and
   mineral wool must be replaced with renewable materials. Adaptation to
   more spatial contexts is encouraged.},
